Searches (drafter): `USER_ID_MATCHES_ASCII_FOLDED` 0/0; `dbReadVerificationsForErasure` 0/0; `keptVerificationIds` 0/0; `rowToVerification` issues 2 (KS-1931, KS-2188), comments 0; `parseJsonb` issues 1 (KS-2188), comments 0; `dbGetVerificationsByUser` issues 4 (KS-1931, KS-2166, KS-2188, KS-2200), comments 4 (KS-1868, KS-1931, KS-2166); `throwOnError` issues 2 (KS-2188, KS-2200), comments 1 (KS-1931); `api/kyc/user/` issues 4 (KS-1882, KS-1931, KS-2166, KS-2185), comments 11; `services/kyc/src/index.ts` issues 19, comments 18 (too wide: read none). The only existing text on the GET read is one sentence in KS-2188's comment ("The GET route's read still fails as a whole on an undecodable row; that is outside this change"); KS-1931's body is about the erasure cascade. KS-2188 closes with PR 2245, so that sentence would leave the board with it: the reason for a ticket and not a comment.
**Proposed title:** `kyc: after PR 2245 the erasure has two untested lines (upper-case id, kept-ids log) and GET /api/kyc/user/:userId loses the whole list to one undecodable row`. Facts (re-read at your own develop tip; the drafter read the line numbers at `42e242166f89`):
- **A, G370-1 (LOW, test gap; product correct):** no cell erases with a NON-lower-case id, by the route path id or the bus payload. Mutant g06 (`translate(user_id, A..Z, a..z) = $1`, the column side only) survives the whole suite; with it an erasure called with an upper-case id would miss every lower-case-stored row, including every row initiated after PR 2245. The shipped SQL folds both sides (gate read it; S1 to S4 exercise the other direction). Gate's follow-up cell: lower-case-stored rows erased via the route with an UPPER path id and caller id, and via the bus with UPPER.
- **B, G370-2 (LOW, test gap):** removing the kept-ids `logger.error` line (g19; at `Blockchain/Dev/services/kyc/src/index.ts` line 469 at the drafter's read) leaves the suite green: P5's "names the kept row id" is satisfied by the cascade handler's own failure line. On the route path the ids would then be logged only by the shared errorHandler (see row A5). The spec says log the ids once at error level.
- **C, the GET read (no severity in the source; stated as a fact, not a rating):** `dbGetVerificationsByUser` at line 223 catches every error and returns an empty list (lines 241 and 242); `rowToVerification` at line 481 resolves the subject key per row (line 491) and runs `parseJsonb(r.checks, [])` at line 550 outside any try; the list is built with `Promise.all`, so one undecodable row loses the whole list; `GET /api/kyc/user/:userId` (line 903) then answers success with count 0. By reading, not run. Gate 334 named two causes of an undecodable row (KS-2188's text); this row's `checks` read is a third, from C 40th. PR 2245's per-row catch fixes the erasure read only.
- **D, C 40th candidate 4:** the `throwOnError` parameter (lines 223 and 241) has no caller passing true since PR 2245 (W1 of that build left it).
- **E, G370-5 (INFO):** initiate now stores the lower-case fold; the GET read stays exact-text (ruling W1 of that build); a caller whose token id is upper-case will not see rows initiated after PR 2245. Near-theoretical (auth issues lower-case uuidv4 ids); named because GET's observed result changes for that caller. Not built or measured.
Say plainly that no PostgreSQL ran in gate 370 and everything here is by reading and the gate's stand-in. Refs KS-2189, KS-2188, KS-1931; no closing word. Priority Low (G370-1 and -2 are rated Low; section C's source gives no rating: W2).

### A4. C 40th row (i): NEW ticket P1 (priority 3, Medium; W2)
Sources: C 40th HANDOVER candidate 1 (line 31); gate 370 "For Kam", item 2 (verdict lines 125 to 127: "Still NOT erased ... The upper-case spelling's `pii_subject_keys` DEK row (a privacy residue; the candidate filing, unmeasured)"). Searches (drafter): `pii_subject_keys` issues 3 (KS-1494, KS-1953, KS-2094; none about the kyc spelling: KS-1953 is originate's lifecycle row, KS-2094 is a swallowed Redis failure), comments 6 (five tickets: KS-1186, KS-173, KS-2189, KS-291, KS-695; KS-2189's 16:22Z comment lists it under "Not covered", so it is named and not ticketed); `subjectDek` issues 2 (KS-1953, KS-963), comments 5; `subjectDeks` issues 1 (KS-1953), comments 2; `subjectDek.ts` issues 1 (KS-1953), comments 1 (KS-695). Read KS-1953 whole before you file: it shares `destroyDek`.
**Proposed title:** `kyc erasure: the subject-key row of an upper-case spelling of a user id survives, so its kyc data stays decryptable in any backup (personal-data residue)`. Say plainly in the first line: **this is a personal-data residue** (the key that decrypts this subject's kyc data is not destroyed). Facts (by reading, at develop `42e242166f89`; nothing run):
- `Blockchain/Dev/packages/shared/src/crypto/subjectDek.ts` keeps and destroys a subject's key by the exact subject text (cache `Map` at line 76; `destroyDek(subjectId)` at line 138; `evict` at line 162).
- kyc resolves a row's key by the row's own spelling: `subjectDeks.getOrCreateDek(v.userId)` at line 167 and `subjectDeks.getDek(r.user_id)` at line 491 of `Blockchain/Dev/services/kyc/src/index.ts`; its erasure evicts only the spelling it was given (`subjectDeks.evict(userId)` at line 463). PR 2245 deletes the rows of every ASCII-case spelling, not that spelling's key row.
- originate's erasure destroys the key by the id it is called with: `Blockchain/Dev/services/originate/src/services/gdprService.ts` line 930 `subjectDeks.destroyDek(userId)`. Which spelling originate receives is UNMEASURED.
- Reach: PR 2245's initiate now stores lower-case, so only rows created BEFORE it under an upper-case spelling can carry such a key; whether any exist is UNMEASURED (auth issues lower-case uuidv4 ids; gate 370 G370-5).
Priority: the sources give no severity line; the drafter proposes **Medium (3)**, the same priority as KS-2189 whose scope this extends, because it is personal data surviving an erasure; **Wednesday rules at the stamp (W2)**. Refs KS-2189, KS-1953; no closing word; not a migration, not Kam's.

